hello my name is cynthia rhodey  and i'm an associate research fellow at  psizer  and today i'm going to be talking to you  about non-clinical covid19 vaccine  development  during a pandemic  i'm going to start off with a brief  background on coronaviruses  and then touch on the target antigen for  most covid19 vaccines that are in  development today  i'll then move on to discuss general  safety considerations for  infectious disease vaccines in general  coded 19 vaccines  and mrna vaccines  then i'll share with you a brief review  of the traditional route of infectious  to vaccines  development and contrast that with a new  paradigm for development  that we have pursued underneath the  coca-19 pandemic  and finally i'll close with some lessons  learned  bronoviruses are a large family of  rna-based viruses that infect birds and  mammals  their envelope single-strand viruses  with one of the largest nucleocapsids  among the rna viruses  the name coronavirus or corona  specifically  meaning crown or halo comes from the  crown-like spikes that cover the surface  of the coronavirus  these viruses primarily cause upper  respiratory  and gastrointestinal disease and this  disease can occur  in many species including  feline infectious peritonitis or fip and  cats  and transmissible gastroenteritis in  pigs  the coronavirus which you're probably  familiar with the structure  the envelope consists of four major  structural proteins  and the little red spikes that are you  can see on the surface  are the spike glycoprotein that mediates  viral entry into the cell  in humans there are four coronaviruses  that are associated with the common cold  two alpha and two beta and more recently  three beta coronaviruses have emerged  that can potentially cause severe  respiratory syndromes in humans  these include sars severe severe  acute respiratory syndrome coronavirus  which emerged in 2002  middle eastern respiratory syndrome  coronavirus  which emerged in 2012 and  the virus that we're dealing with today  sars kobe 2  which emerged in china in 2019  among the coronaviruses  despite protein glycoprotein binds to  a variety of receptors  in particular for the human cyrus kobe  co coronavirus the original as well as  sars kobe  ii the spike protein of these viruses  bind to the angiotensin converting  enzyme 2 or h2  this slide shows an image of the spy  protein  in more detail it is a trimeric  protein that consists of three main  subunits  excuse me i'm going to go back and um  with the small um  receptor binding domain or rbd  pointing down and binding to the blue  ace2  receptor on the host cell service  it is only one of these receptor binding  domains  they fluctuate in the up and down  position and  when in the prefusion conformation  the spike protein only has one of these  three  receptor binding domains that is in the  up position  now i'm going to transition to telling  you a little bit more  about general considerations for safety  evaluation  of infectious disease vaccines the  toxicology  assessment of these types of vaccines is  intended to evaluate the safety of both  the vaccine components as well as the  associated  immune response off-target or severe  expected toxicity  is generally not accepted as these  vaccines may be administered to millions  or even billions of healthy healthy  people  although exceptions may be made based on  the risk benefits  profile  there are a number of major regulatory  guidelines for infectious disease  vaccines  and i've listed a few of them here but  there may also be regional and  disease-specific guidances to consult  in general as infectious disease  vaccines  are most likely targeting a foreign  antigen  that should not be cross-reactive with  any antigens  present in humans non-clinical safety  studies  that are performed for these types of  vaccines are generally  smaller are generally smaller intact in  number  than for a typical small molecule or  biologic  in general a repeat dose toxicity  in a single species is sufficient  and then depending on the target  population a developmental  and reproductive toxicity or dart study  may also be conducted in a single  species  finally depending on the specific  vaccine modality  and if you're using any novel adjuvants  or excipients in the vaccine  other non-clinical safety related  studies may be conducted  such as biodistribution neurovirulence  etc  some viral infections as well as  vaccinations  and this includes for in regards to  viral infections things like flabby  viruses  measles or even respiratory synthesial  virus  can illustrate neutralizing as well as  non-neutralizing  enhancing or non-enhancing antibodies  if more non-neutralizing or  sub-neutralizing antibodies are  generated  that do not clear or block the infection  then antibody dependent enhancement or  ade  can occur occasionally non-neutralizing  antibodies  and or complement components bind to the  virus  in the case of a viral infection and  promote phagocytic  cellular entry via the fc gamma  or complement receptors  examples of this have been reported with  dengue fever which is a flavia virus  but have also been seen with feline  coronavirus  and feline coronavirus vaccines in cats  with fip  and inconsistently in some animal models  the hamster and rhesus macaque of  sars coronavirus thus ede  is a theoretical safety concern when  developing a vaccine  for sars kobe 2.  another point to consider when thinking  about ade  is that enhanced disease may also be  driven by the generation  of the th2 rather than a th1 response  to the vaccine antigen  so what is the risk of ade with vaccines  targeting sars cody2 it appeared that  the risk  was low based on several factors first  expectations were that covid19 vaccines  would produce high neutralizing antibody  titers  and low levels of non-neutralizing  antibodies  as more code 19 cases occurred there has  been no  evidence so far of enhanced disease  after cyroscob2 infection and  re-exposure  or treatment with convalescent plasma  and coronavirus enhanced disease has so  far  only been demonstrated as i mentioned  before with the feline coronavirus  which is an alpha coronavirus while sars  cov2  is a beta coronavirus  however while the likelihood was low  it is still a potential risk and  monitoring for ade  was still warranted so non-clinical  efficacy models  have been used to assess the potential  for ade  and in addition clinical trials included  mechanisms to identify  signs of ade to date there hasn't been  any reported  in addition vaccine clinical trials have  shown a strong th1  rather than a th2 response  now let's talk a bit about a vaccine  modality  that i've become very familiar with the  mrna-based vaccine  how do these vaccines work the mrna  encoding the antigen of choice in this  case despite  a glycoprotein locked into a prefusion  conformation  is encapsulated into a lipid  nanoparticle or lnp  the lipid nanoparticle increases the  half-life of the mrna compared to  induction of naked mrna and also  works to increase cellular uptake  once the lnp is taken up by the host  cell  it travels through the endosomal  pathways and eventually releases  its contents the rna into the size fall  the mrna is then translated and the  protein antigen  is expressed depending on the signals  contained within the rna and the express  protein  the protein itself may be expressed on  the cell surface  or the protein may be processed by the  proteasome  and peptide fragments presented via the  major histocompatibility complexes or  mhc  this slide lists some of the safety  considerations for an  mrna lnp vaccine first  mrna is non-infectious and  non-integrating  it is degraded shortly after engineering  the cell  but can stimulate dna immune system via  toll-like receptors such as  trl tlr7  mrna can be modified to contain  modified nucleosides many of which are  naturally occurring such as  pseudo-uridine  which work to dampen that innate immune  response  finally where does the rna go and where  is the protein expressed  this is driven by the lnp which acts as  the delivery device for the rna  lnps generally consists of structural  lipids  such as cholesterol and phospholipids in  ionizable lipid  the complexes with the mrna and a  pegulated lipid to allow for better  stability in half-life  of course if any of the components of  the lnp  now the lipids are used safety and  anatomy assessment  will likely need to be conducted  this figure shows the traditional  approach to infectious vaccine  development and as you can see  that can take many years sequential  investments are made along the way  as more confidence is gained in the  vaccine safety  profile and efficacy this approach was  obviously not going to work to stem the  world wide spread of sarcoid ii  and its associated disease to the  dentine  in the u.s the first step to changing  the approach to vaccine development  occurred with the deluxe declaration of  a public health emergency  related to covid19 which occurred in in  february of last year  this allows the option to authorize use  of unapproved products  the criteria for granting an emergency  use authorization  includes the disease must be serious or  life-threatening  the vaccine or treatment must show  evidence of efficacy  the risk benefit analysis must favor the  use of the vaccine or treatment  and there should be no adequate approved  or available alternative  in addition to the eua a precedent for a  rapid approach to vaccine development  during a public health emergency was  established by the who  in 2018 when evaluating the potential  ebola vaccines  this approach emphasized the use of  platform data  which could be used alone or with  supporting  interim in life data to enter phase 1  clinical trials  while the who focused on viral  vector-based vaccines  i've expanded the potential definition  of a platform as follows  a platform is a modality in which  components are comparable  with the exception of the antigen itself  so that would mean the same adjuvant  excipient the same dna viral vector  or plasmid or rna platform  as well as the same delivery device  later guidances issued by the fda  specifically on coca-19 vaccines  which are included here on the slide  also supported  the use of platform data as a way to  accelerate development  in 2020 other global guidances and  initiatives supported rapid covet 19  vaccine development including  a workshop by the international  coalition of medicine's  regulatory authorities that supported  use of platform data  to allow for rapid entry into the  clinical into clinical trials  and from the who outlining a pathway  from  an emergency use listing for coding 19  vaccines  as an example of how this approach was  used  and in particular for our pfizer beyond  tech  coca-19 vaccine bnt-162b2 or community  this slide shows the non-clinical safety  related studies that were supplied to  health authorities to support the ind  or cta compared with the data that was  supplied to support the eua  or maa for the ind and cta  we leverage rna platform non-clinical  and clinical data  from candidate vaccines targeting other  indications  in addition to interim dosing phase data  from an ongoing repeat dose toxicity  study  with a number of coded 19 vaccine  candidates  this was supplemented with some protein  expression  biodistribution data for the eua  and maa we provided final results from  repeat dose toxicity studies that have  been conducted  timing of the dart study results in the  situation of the eua or in the case of  the maa  an audited draft report from that dart  study  in addition data from an nhp  immunization and challenge study  which was our non-clinical assessment  for enhanced  enhanced disease which was provided  protein and lnp biodistribution data  as well as a small admin package for the  novel lipids that we use  in our lnp  although we were able to utilize an  accelerated approach  i must emphasize that there were no  compromises made  to the safety evaluation of the vaccine  non-clinical safety data including  platform data  and immunogenicity data was provided to  health authorities  prior to entry into the clinic  many of the activities were performed in  parallel  on an accelerated path versus sequential  the more traditional paths that i  discussed or discussed earlier  and these included non-clinical clinical  and manufacturing  activities as well as others  as well as we engaged in constant  communication  with regulatory authorities on the  timing of delivery of non-clinical and  clinical data to support  filings as well as ongoing activities  this slide showing a snapshot of the new  york times copied 19 vaccine tracker  illustrates how far we've come with  approximately 10 vaccines already  authorized or approved in various  countries around the world  and some or more close to it giving us  the tools  to tackle the current coded 19 pandemic  in summary what i learned from my  experience helping to develop  a covid19 vaccine during the ongoing  pandemic is that early and frequent  interactions  with health authorities was critical  platform data can support clinical trial  initiation  and this data may be supplemented with  interim data  for less well-characterized platforms  any safety data from  candidate vaccines or related vaccines  in humans  may be relevant and finally it also  leads me to wonder  if there are any other areas where we  can use these lessons  such as for severely debilitating and  life-threatening indications  my final slide acknowledges only a few  of the other pfizer and biontech  colleagues that were involved in the  non-clinical development of bnt 162b2  as it's always a team effort thank you  very much

